          "tool_use_id": "toolu_015NRgtVSy9PkdrdDEJnFLtu",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022concepts/retrieval-patterns.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Retrieval Patterns\\ndescription: Techniques for combining and ranking results from multiple retrieval methods, including hybrid search that merges sparse lexical and dense vector results.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Retrieval\\n  - concept/Ranking\\n  - concept/RAG\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Single external source on hybrid retrieval; corroborates existing page\\u0027s mention of reciprocal rank fusion.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\n## Hybrid Retrieval\\n\\nHybrid retrieval combines sparse lexical search (e.g., BM25) with dense vector search, then merges the two ranked result sets\\u2014often via reciprocal rank fusion\\u2014to capture both exact term matches and semantic similarity.\\n\\nHybrid retrieval consistently outperforms either method alone on queries mixing rare identifiers (error codes, product SKUs) with natural language intent. It has become the default retrieval pattern in production RAG systems.\\n\\n### Merging Strategies\\n\\n**Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF)** is a common technique for combining results from sparse and dense retrievers. It takes the rank position of each result in each result set and computes a normalized score, allowing principled fusion without requiring the individual scores to be on the same scale.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: Source on hybrid retrieval patterns in production RAG systems (eval://update-over-duplicate/sample-02).\\n\u0022}"
        },
